From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EvilMetaMonkey <hood.mj85@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 01:55:03 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Let Git decide what is ignored inside a Git work tree

When the workspace root is inside a Git work tree, the protected file
list is git ls-files --cached --others --exclude-standard (run with -C
<root>, NUL-separated, converted to native paths). That honours nested
.gitignore files, negation, character classes, info/exclude,
core.excludesFile, and tracked files that match a pattern. DMC's default
directories, .dmcignore, link and size rules still apply on top.
Gitlinks, submodules and nested repositories are excluded.

Git is required in that mode: if it cannot be found (PATH, then
/usr/bin, /opt/homebrew/bin, /usr/local/bin, or Git for Windows) or run,
begin records git_unavailable/git_failed and close leaves the checkpoint
open, instead of falling back to the subset parser. Outside Git the
subset parser and its refusals are unchanged.

The policy fingerprint (version 2 in Git mode) covers the mode and every
ignore source Git reads: the effective .gitignore files, info/exclude
and core.excludesFile. A .gitignore that a tool writes into a directory
Git already ignores (pytest, venv) is not a source.

The watcher checks Git once per drain with git check-ignore --stdin -z,
never per event. status and doctor print the ignore policy; doctor
reports an unusable one as an issue.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
